  "text": "Allophone: A phonological variant of a phoneme that occurs in specific phonological environments.\n\nAnaphora: A pronoun or other word that refers back to an antecedent in the discourse.\n\nApocope: The dropping or omission of a phoneme or syllable at the end of a word.\n\nArbitrariness: The principle that the relationship between a linguistic sign\u2019s signifier and signified is not necessary or logical.\n\nAssimilation: A phonological process where a sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to speak and understand two languages.\n\nCode-switching: The practice of alternating between two or more languages or dialects in the course of conversation.\n\nComplementizer: A grammatical element that introduces a clause, such as \"that\" in English.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced by obstructing the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster: A sequence of two or more consonants occurring together without a vowel in between.\n\nConsonant harmony: A phonological process where consonants in a word change to match each other in some feature.\n\nConsonant mutation: A phonological process involving the change of a consonant in certain environments, often found in Celtic languages.\n\nCopula: A grammatical word that links the subject of a sentence to a complement, such as \"be\" in English.\n\nDiacritic: A mark added to a letter to change its pronunciation or meaning, such as the acute accent in French.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and glides to another.\n\nDolgoch: A term used in some Slavic languages to refer to a long vowel.\n\nDialect: A variety of a language distinguished by pronunciation, vocabulary, and grammar.\n\nEjective consonant: A consonant produced with a burst of air from the glottis.\n\nFricative: A consonant produced by forcing air through a narrow channel, creating friction.\n\nGesture: A movement of the hands, arms, or body used to convey meaning in communication.\n\nGlottal stop: A consonant produced by blocking the airflow at the glottis.\n\nGrapheme: A written symbol that represents a phoneme in a writing system.\n\nHigh vowel: A vowel produced with the tongue raised high in the mouth.\n\nIambic: A metrical pattern consisting of an unstressed syllable followed by a stressed syllable.\n\nInfix: A morpheme inserted within a word, typically in the middle, rather than at the beginning or end.\n\nLaryngealization: A phonological process involving the modification of a sound by the larynx.\n\nLexeme: A unit of vocabulary that includes all its inflected forms.\n\nLingua franca: A language that serves as a common means of communication between speakers of different native languages.\n\nLinguistic relativity: The hypothesis that language influences thought and perception.\n\nLexicon: The vocabulary of a language, including all words and their meanings.\n\nLocus of stress: The position of stress in a word, which can affect meaning or pronunciation.\n\nMorpheme: The smallest meaningful unit in a language.\n\nNasal consonant: A consonant produced with the soft palate lowered, allowing air to escape through the nose.\n\nNasalization: A phonological process where a vowel or consonant is produced with nasal resonance.\n\nNoun class: A category in some languages where nouns are grouped based on grammatical features.\n\nObstruent: A consonant produced with a complete or partial obstruction of airflow.\n\nOligonymy: A term referring to a language with a limited number of words.\n\nOnomatopoeia: A word that phonetically imitates or suggests the sound it represents.\n\nOrthography: The system of writing in a language, including",
